Improved question: “Which hidden readiness gates—levain ripe, autolyse finished, dough mixed long enough for folds, bulk fermentation complete before dividing, preshaped rounds rested before final shaping, retardation finished, dutch oven hot at the same moment the loaves come out, the single dutch oven free between bakes, and cooling rack capacity—must block or overlap, and how do they change the dependency graph?”

Answer and trigger revisions: the text implies several “wait for all” gates that are not explicit chains. Add the levain to the autolysed dough only when both levain fermentation and autolyse are complete. Do not divide until the last fold and the bulk fermentation are both complete. Shape only after the preshaped rest is complete. Start the oven/dutch-oven preheat 45 minutes before the minimum retardation finishes, and do not score/lower the first loaf until both retardation and preheat are complete. The two loaves are serialized through the single dutch oven by putting every scoring, baking, and re-heating step on one sequential dutch-oven track while allowing the first loaf to cool on a two-slot rack during the second bake setup. No additional arbitrary timed activity is needed; adding one would invent durations not given by the text.

